From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 12:19:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] assignment: parse an assignment chain in a loop

AssignmentExpression parsed `target =` and then recursed into itself for
the right-hand side, so `a = (b = (c = ...))` kept two of its frames on
the stack per nesting level, ~1 KiB each on wasm, and `a = b = c = ...`
recursed once per `=`. Minified code is full of both; reCAPTCHA's
constructor nests the parenthesized form ~75 deep.

Each step now returns either the chain's final value or a pending
target, and the targets fold from the right once the value is parsed:
the same nodes, anonymous function names and errors as before (281 npm
bundles and 200k generated scripts digest identically).

Deepest nesting boa-bench compiles, after the precedence-parsing patch:
`o.p=(o.p=(...))` 174 -> 230, `((...))` 209 -> 230, `a=b=...=1` 1066 ->
2254. Compile suite unchanged.
